  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nA slow-moving compact wave along an elongated trough from \nArkansas to southern Indiana will drift northeastward across \nIndiana through tonight. Upper-level clouds associated with the \ntrough will continue to filter northward over the area through \ntonight. Meanwhile, a cold will track across the area late \nSaturday morning through the afternoon under notable surface-\nbased capping. Given a rather dry antecedent airmass north of \nthe trough and ahead of the cold front and a lack of organized \nlarge-scale forcing, precip-free conditions are expected through\nSaturday. The only exception is for the potential for a few \nshowers nearing/brushing the extreme southeast CWA (i.e. Benton \nCounty) late tonight.\n\nA mid-level trough crossing the western Great Lakes will \ninteract with the stalling cold front across central Illinois \nSunday before ridging quickly builds northeastward Sunday night \ninto Monday. Optimal mid-level jet placement over and to the \nnortheast of the area in conjunction with broad low-level \nisentropic ascent over the frontal surface should yield several \nbands of rain and/or scattered showers with perhaps some \nembedded thunderstorms Sunday afternoon into Monday morning. \nSufficient moisture will be a limiting factor for precip \ncoverage and intensity, so at least some of the area will likely\nremain dry during this period.\n\nAn aforementioned cold front will lift back northward as a warm\nfront Monday afternoon and night in response to a strong \nsurface low crossing far south-central Canada. An associated \nocclusion with the system's cold front and existing warm front \nwill ultimately lead to a stalled front near or over the \nforecast area Tuesday into Wednesday. Persistent low-level \nmoisture advection within an airmass characterized by PWATS of \n1.7 to as high as 2 inches will likely lead to several rounds of\nheavy rain/showers and convection in this corridor, with an \nincreased flooding potential if multiple rounds of precip align \nover the same area. Continue to monitor for updates as the \nforecast location of any persistent rain corridor becomes more \nrefined.\n\nThe front will attempt to shift south late in the week as high \npressure builds southward across the Great Lakes region. Have \nmaintained chance PoPs Thursday and Friday as the showers would \nlikely continue over the area if the front struggles remains \nstalled or only drifts southward with time. \n\nKluber",
